# An exported class is the route of its doors: building it and each public method, each called by the entry: the
# methods run on the caller's instance, `Store` from beyond, and what the building makes goes back to the caller. The
# puts are the clean side's: `rows.push(Row { reading: reading.clone() })` (`clone`, which std derives, is no step)
# makes `rows` anew, so the Reading reaches what is written at `Disk(Store.path)`. `get` reads the lines itself and
# gives them back as they are, of no type of the code's: no Reading comes back out of the place it went into.

